---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import RingSizer from '../components/RingSizer.astro';
import { NOINDEX_SITE, SITE_NAME, SITE_URL } from '../config';
import { CHART_ROWS, AVERAGE_US_SIZE, diameterFromUs, fromDiameter } from '../data/ringSizes';
import { FAQS, faqPageSchema, howToSchema, webAppSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../data/faq';

/* The chart below is generated, never typed. Half-size steps from US 3 to 13 —
   the range actually sold. Quarter steps live on /ring-size-chart when it ships. */
const chart = CHART_ROWS.filter(
  (r) => r.usNumeric !== null && r.usNumeric % 0.5 === 0 && r.usNumeric <= 13,
);

/* Used in prose so the numbers can never drift from the conversion engine. */
const six = fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(6));
const smallest = fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(3));
const largest = fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(13));

const inRange = (r: typeof six) => r.circumferenceIn.toFixed(1);

/* Structured data. Built from the same modules the page renders from, so the
   visible answer and the schema answer can never disagree. */
const jsonLd = [
  webAppSchema(SITE_URL, SITE_NAME),
  howToSchema(SITE_URL),
  faqPageSchema(),
  breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Ring Sizer', url: SITE_URL }]),
];
---
<Layout
  title="Ring Sizer — Measure Your Ring Size on Screen | Ring Size Tool"
  description="Free ring sizer. Measure your ring size at home with a bank card and your screen — no printer, no ring sizer needed. US, UK, EU, Japan and India sizes instantly."
  noindex={NOINDEX_SITE}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-5 py-10 sm:py-14">

    <!-- ── Hero ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Left-aligned and deliberately short. This is an instrument, not a
         pitch: the calibration card has to be reachable without scrolling.

         Removed on purpose (27 Aug) — the page had become the standard
         AI-landing-page template: a circular icon badge, an uppercase
         letterspaced eyebrow used twice, a centred subhead and a symmetric
         two-card "what you'll need" grid with pill badges. Seven interchangeable
         parts that appear on every generated site this year. The brass word in
         the h1 stays; that is a deliberate DESIGN.md decision, not a template tic.

         The h1 wording is the "without a ring sizer" cluster — 9 keyword
         variants, the largest in the set, and no competitor leads with it. -->
    <header>
      <h1 class="max-w-[18ch] text-[clamp(2.25rem,5.5vw,3.5rem)]">
        Find your ring size <span class="text-accent">without a ring sizer</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[58ch] text-lg text-text-muted">
        Hold a bank card against your screen to calibrate, then measure a ring you already own —
        or your finger with a strip of paper. Nothing is uploaded.
      </p>

      <!-- The one claim no competitor can copy, because it is what we found
           rather than what we assert. Links to the section that proves it. -->
      <p class="mt-6 border-l-2 border-accent py-1 pl-4 text-sm text-text-muted">
        Four ring size sites give <strong class="text-text">three different UK answers</strong> for
        US&nbsp;6. Every number here is computed from the published standard instead.{' '}
        <a href="#disagree" class="whitespace-nowrap text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2"
          >See the arithmetic</a
        >
      </p>

      <!-- Was a two-card grid. It is a prerequisites line; it reads as one. -->
      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-text-faint">
        <span class="text-text-muted">You need</span> any bank card — every one is
        85.60&nbsp;×&nbsp;53.98&nbsp;mm (ISO/IEC&nbsp;7810), which is what makes this accurate.
        A ring that already fits helps; a strip of paper works just as well.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <RingSizer />
    </div>

    <!-- ── Is this number normal? ──────────────────────────────────────────
         Queries: "is size 7 a large ring" · "is a size 7 ring big for a girl"
                  "what is the most common ring size" · "average ring size"
         Three separate PAA entries ask this. No audited competitor answers it. -->
    <section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="normal">
      <h2 id="normal" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">Is your size normal?</h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Almost certainly. Rings are sold from about US {smallest.us} to US {largest.us}, and most
        adults land in a narrow band inside that.
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll mt-6">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[30rem] text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="sr-only">Most common and typical ring sizes</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-surface-sunk">
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-l-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">&nbsp;</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Most common</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Typical range</th>
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-r-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">Inner diameter</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">Women</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">US {AVERAGE_US_SIZE.women}</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">US 4 – 8½</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">{`${fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(4)).diameterMm.toFixed(1)} – ${fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(8.5)).diameterMm.toFixed(1)} mm`}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">Men</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">US {AVERAGE_US_SIZE.men}</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">US 8 – 13</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">{`${fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(8)).diameterMm.toFixed(1)} – ${largest.diameterMm.toFixed(1)} mm`}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        One caveat worth stating plainly: there is no census of finger sizes. These figures come from
        what jewellers stock and sell, so they describe ring <em>buyers</em>, not people. Treat them
        as a sanity check, not a verdict. A size 4 and a size 12 are both ordinary.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        So <strong class="text-text">US 7 is not a large ring.</strong> It is one size above the
        commonest women's size and two below the commonest men's — squarely in the middle of what
        every jeweller keeps in stock.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Methods ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Query cluster: "how to measure ring size without a ring sizer" and its
         9 variants — the largest cluster in the research, and the site's pitch. -->
    <section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="methods">
      <h2 id="methods" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">
        Three ways to measure without a ring sizer
      </h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        All three end at the same number: the <strong class="text-text">inner diameter</strong> of the
        ring, in millimetres. Everything else — US, UK, EU, Japan — is arithmetic on that one figure.
        Get the millimetres right and the rest cannot be wrong.
      </p>

      <!-- Card layout adopted from the Aurelian system: an identity panel on the
           left, materials and numbered steps on the right. Clearer than prose
           for a procedure someone follows with their hands busy. -->
      <ol class="mt-10 space-y-4" role="list">

        <li class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface sm:flex">
          <div class="flex shrink-0 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-2 border-border bg-surface-sunk px-6 py-6 sm:w-56 sm:border-r">
            <svg width="30" height="30" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                 stroke-width="1.25" class="text-accent-bright" aria-hidden="true">
              <circle cx="12" cy="14" r="7" /><circle cx="12" cy="14" r="3.5" />
              <path d="M9.5 5.5 12 2.5l2.5 3" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            </svg>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.1em] text-text-faint uppercase">Method 1</p>
            <p class="text-center font-display text-xl leading-tight">A ring that fits</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-accent-text">Most accurate</p>
          </div>

          <div class="flex-1 px-5 py-5 sm:px-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.1em] text-text-faint uppercase">Materials needed</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-text-muted">A ring that already fits the finger you are measuring, and any bank card.</p>

            <p class="mt-5 text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.1em] text-text-faint uppercase">Steps</p>
            <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-text-muted">
              <li>Calibrate the screen above with a bank card. Every card is 85.60 mm wide worldwide
                (ISO/IEC 7810 ID-1), so it doubles as a ruler you already own.</li>
              <li>Lay the ring flat on the screen over the circle.</li>
              <li>Grow the circle until it just touches the ring's <strong class="text-text">inner</strong>
                edge — the hole, not the outside of the band.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
              Matching the outer edge instead is the single most common mistake, and on a 2 mm band it
              makes you two full sizes too big.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface sm:flex">
          <div class="flex shrink-0 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-2 border-border bg-surface-sunk px-6 py-6 sm:w-56 sm:border-r">
            <svg width="30" height="30" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                 stroke-width="1.25" class="text-accent-bright" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M3 8.5h18v7H3z" /><path d="M7 8.5v7M11 8.5v7M15 8.5v7M19 8.5v7" opacity="0.5" />
            </svg>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.1em] text-text-faint uppercase">Method 2</p>
            <p class="text-center font-display text-xl leading-tight">A strip of paper</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-text-faint">No ring needed</p>
          </div>

          <div class="flex-1 px-5 py-5 sm:px-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.1em] text-text-faint uppercase">Materials needed</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-text-muted">A strip of paper about 5 &times; 100 mm, a pen, and a ruler with millimetre markings.</p>

            <p class="mt-5 text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.1em] text-text-faint uppercase">Steps</p>
            <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-text-muted">
              <li>Cut a strip about 5 mm wide and 100 mm long. Paper only — not thread, not string.</li>
              <li>Wrap it around the base of the finger. Snug enough that it will not slide off, loose
                enough to turn.</li>
              <li>Mark where the end overlaps, unwrap, and measure the flat strip to the mark.</li>
              <li>Enter that length under <strong class="text-text">Measure my finger</strong>. It is the
                circumference; the tool divides by π to get the diameter.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
              Check the number before you trust it. A finger measures roughly {inRange(smallest)} to
              {' '}{inRange(largest)} inches around ({smallest.circumferenceMm.toFixed(0)}–{largest.circumferenceMm.toFixed(0)} mm).
              If you got 7 inches, you have measured a wrist.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface sm:flex">
          <div class="flex shrink-0 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-2 border-border bg-surface-sunk px-6 py-6 sm:w-56 sm:border-r">
            <svg width="30" height="30" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                 stroke-width="1.25" class="text-accent-bright" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M8 3.5v13M16 3.5v13" /><path d="M8 20.5h8" />
              <path d="M8 10h8" stroke-dasharray="1 2" />
            </svg>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.1em] text-text-faint uppercase">Method 3</p>
            <p class="text-center font-display text-xl leading-tight">You know the diameter</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-text-faint">No calibration</p>
          </div>

          <div class="flex-1 px-5 py-5 sm:px-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.1em] text-text-faint uppercase">Materials needed</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-text-muted">A diameter in millimetres — from a jeweller, a product listing, or a caliper.</p>

            <p class="mt-5 text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.1em] text-text-faint uppercase">Steps</p>
            <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-text-muted">
              <li>Enter it under <strong class="text-text">I know the size</strong>.</li>
              <li>Skip step 1 entirely — no calibration is needed when the millimetres already exist.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </li>

      </ol>

      <h3 class="mt-10 text-lg">What not to do</h3>
      <ul class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-text-muted">
        <li><strong class="text-text">String or thread.</strong> It stretches, and it tightens as you
          pull. Both errors push the same way, so you land small.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-text">A printed chart at the wrong scale.</strong> Browsers and
          printers rescale by default. If the page has no check square you can measure, the chart
          is decoration.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-text">Measuring at the end of a hot day.</strong> See the section
          below.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-text">Zooming after calibrating.</strong> The tool sizes the circle
          in pixels. Change the zoom and every result shifts.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- ── The chart ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Queries: "ring size chart" · "ring size in mm" · "uk to us ring size"
         The #1 competitor gates its full chart behind a PDF download. Ours is
         on the page, and generated from the same engine as the tool. -->
    <section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="chart">
      <h2 id="chart" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">Ring size chart</h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Half sizes from US {smallest.us} to US {largest.us}, computed from the standards listed
        below — not copied from another chart. Nothing here is behind a download.
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll mt-6">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[42rem] text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="sr-only">
            Ring size conversion chart: US, UK, EU, Japan and India with diameter and circumference
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-surface-sunk">
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-l-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">US / CA</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">UK / AU</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">EU / ISO</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Japan</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">India</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Diameter</th>
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-r-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">Circumference</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="tabular">
            {chart.map((r) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-border">
                <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-2.5 font-medium text-text">{r.us}</th>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.uk}</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.eu}</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.jp}</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.in === null ? '—' : r.in}</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.diameterMm.toFixed(2)} mm</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.circumferenceMm.toFixed(2)} mm</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-sm text-text-faint">
        UK sizes run as long stretches of half sizes. That is correct, not a bug: a US step is
        2.55 mm of circumference and a UK step is 1.25 mm, so the two scales never line up.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ── The thing only we know ──────────────────────────────────────────
         Queries: "ring size conversion" · "uk to us ring size" · "ring size in mm"
         This is the page's original contribution — the discrepancy we verified. -->
    <section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="disagree">
      <h2 id="disagree" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">
        Why two ring size charts give you different answers
      </h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Because most of them are copies of copies. We checked the sites currently ranking for this
        search, and they contradict each other — and in one case themselves.
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-text-muted">
        <li>For a {six.diameterMm.toFixed(2)} mm inner diameter, three ranking sites give three
          different UK sizes: L, L½ and M.</li>
        <li>The page ranking first for <em>ring size calculator</em> reports 16.50 mm as EU 52 on its
          millimetre slider and the same 1.65 cm as EU 51.8 on its centimetre slider.</li>
        <li>Its two on-page charts disagree with each other about US 5 — one says 16.2 mm, the other
          15.7 mm — and the first one skips sizes 6 and 8 altogether.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        So we do not keep a chart. We keep one number — inner diameter in millimetres — and derive
        everything else from it with the published formula. Two of our numbers cannot disagree,
        because there is only ever one number.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-10 text-lg">The arithmetic, in full</h3>

      <div class="table-scroll mt-4">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[36rem] text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="sr-only">Conversion rules and their sources</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-surface-sunk">
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-l-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">System</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Rule</th>
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-r-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">Source</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">US / Canada</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">diameter = 0.8128 n + 11.633 mm</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">de facto — no standards body</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">UK / Australia</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">A = 37.5 mm circumference, +1.25 mm a letter</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">BS EN 28653:1993</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">EU</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">size = inner circumference in mm</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">ISO 8653:2016</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">FR / IT / ES / CH</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">circumference − 40</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">same scale as ISO, renumbered</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">Japan</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 tabular">size 1 = 13 mm diameter, +⅓ mm a size</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">JIS S 4700:2022</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">India</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3">no formula — lookup table</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">no standard exists</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        You will often see the US rule written as
        <span class="tabular">circumference = 2.55 n + 36.5 mm</span>. That is the same rule, rounded:
        multiply the diameter formula by π and you get 2.5535 n + 36.549. We use the unrounded form,
        which is why our millimetres carry two decimals rather than one.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-10 text-lg">Why our UK letter may differ from your jeweller&rsquo;s</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Ask four ring size sites what US 6 is in UK letters and you get three answers:
        <span class="tabular">L</span>, <span class="tabular">L½</span> and <span class="tabular">M</span>.
        We checked. Ours is <span class="tabular">L½</span>, taken straight from BS EN 28653:1993 —
        size A is 37.5 mm of inner circumference and every letter adds 1.25 mm.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        The disagreement is not arithmetic. Those charts are internally consistent too: one US
        quarter-size is 0.638 mm of circumference and one UK half-letter is 0.625 mm, so the two
        scales genuinely track each other. The gap comes from the <em>letter sequence</em>. British
        scales do not all include the same half sizes — the chart we compared omits I½ — and one
        missing half-step shifts everything above it by half a letter.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        We could have quietly shifted our formula to match the most common chart. We have not,
        because that would mean replacing a standard with somebody&rsquo;s copy of a copy. Instead
        we give you the letter the standard produces and tell you it can vary. If your jeweller
        works from a different chart, hand them the circumference in millimetres — that number
        means the same thing on every chart in the world.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-10 text-lg">India, France, Italy, Spain and Brazil are one scale</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        This is the part no other ring size page seems to say out loud. France, Italy, Spain,
        Switzerland and Brazil all number rings the same way: take the inner circumference in
        millimetres and subtract 40. Brazil&rsquo;s standard, ABNT NBR 16058, is aligned to
        ISO&nbsp;8653 and gives the same answer — the Brazilian trade rule is literally &ldquo;add 40
        to the size to get the perimeter&rdquo;. So a French size 12, an Italian 12 and a Brazilian
        aro 12 are the same physical ring. Our table shows them in separate columns because that is
        what people search for, but the number will always match.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-10 text-lg">Where we are genuinely unsure: India</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        India has no sizing standard — there is no BIS specification for ring sizes, so there is
        nothing to be wrong about. What we did instead of copying somebody&rsquo;s chart was compare
        the published tables of four Indian jewellers: Jewelove, RishiRich Jewels, Gems Mart
        Jewellers and Sukkhi. All four turn out to use the same &ldquo;circumference minus 40&rdquo;
        rule as France and Brazil, and they agree on every row they publish — Sukkhi&rsquo;s table
        runs from size 1 to 37 and fits the rule exactly. That is what we compute.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        The catch is Tanishq, the largest jeweller in India, whose published chart runs roughly one
        size smaller than that convention. We could not confirm the offset across their full range,
        because Tanishq blocks requests from Pakistan, where this site is built — so we are telling
        you what we know rather than papering over it. Every competitor we audited prints one Indian
        number as though it were settled. We print ours next to the diameter in millimetres, and
        that millimetre figure is the one to give your jeweller. It is the only unambiguous answer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Edge cases ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Queries: "ring size knuckle bigger than finger" · "does ring size change"
                  "wide band ring size" · "how to find someone's ring size secretly"
                  "which rings cannot be resized" -->
    <section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="cases">
      <h2 id="cases" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">Cases the number alone won't cover</h2>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg">Your knuckle is wider than the base of your finger</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Measure both, and take the size that clears the knuckle. A ring that fits the base perfectly
        but will not pass the knuckle is not wearable; a ring that clears the knuckle and sits a
        little loose is. If the gap between the two is more than about a full size, a jeweller can
        fit sizing beads inside the band — that solves it properly, and no calculator can.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg">The band is wide</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        A wide band covers more of the finger and feels tighter at the same diameter. The usual
        advice is to add a quarter to a half size above roughly 6 mm of width.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        We will not dress that up as a calculation. There is no formula for it — the correction
        depends on how soft the finger is and how rounded the inside edge of the band is, and no
        standard covers either. Anyone giving you an exact figure for this has invented it. Try the
        half size up if the band is wide and the fit is borderline.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg">Fingers change size during the day</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Warmth, salt, exercise and altitude all swell fingers; cold shrinks them. The swing is real
        enough to cross a half size, which is why a ring can spin in the morning and stick by
        evening. Measure at room temperature, in the middle of the day, and if you get two different
        answers, take the larger.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg">You are sizing someone else's finger, secretly</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Borrow a ring they already wear on the right finger and measure it with method 1 above. The
        finger matters: a ring worn on the right hand is usually a quarter to a half size different
        from the same finger on the left, and non-ring fingers are not comparable at all. If you can
        only get a ring from the wrong finger, buy the ring resizable and size it after.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg">Some rings cannot be resized at all</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Eternity rings set with stones the whole way round, tension settings, and rings in tungsten,
        titanium, ceramic or stainless steel are effectively fixed — there is no plain metal to cut
        and no safe way to work the material. For any of those, and for an engagement ring, confirm
        with a jeweller before you buy. An on-screen measurement is a good estimate; it is not a
        fitting.
      </p>
    </section>

